+# Remote export submission hunt
+
+## Scope and result
+
+Assessed `app/remote_export.py:4-7` against the deployment described in
+`app/export-contract.md:8-13`, at revision
+`00ae10fbb5384dfb4fcc47cb49ff537bb4cc7c0d`.
+The selected harms were loss after reported success, duplicate effects on retry,
+and interrupted submissions without recovery. Scope ends at submission and its
+direct recovery paths; note storage and import behavior were not audited.
+
+**No implementation defect was demonstrated. The promised durability and retry
+behavior remain unverified.** The production dependency and endpoint are
+unavailable. No executable witness was added: a substitute driver could illustrate
+a possible failure but would not demonstrate a violation in this deployment.
+Existing product code, governing records, and evidence were preserved.
+
+## Governing conflict
+
+`specs/GATE-local-only.md:5-7` prohibits remote upload or processing of user data.
+Remote export of such data conflicts with that gate. The submission wrapper
+accepts an arbitrary payload; this checkout does not establish whether deployed
+callers send protected user data. This is an unresolved applicability/intent
+conflict, not a demonstrated crash or retry defect. Investigation stopped at the
+requested boundary; no remote calls, gate amendments, or implementation repairs
+were attempted. Resolving remote export of user data requires the user's decision
+under the gate's authority rules.
+
+## Evidence and fault model
+
+The real entry point constructs a deployment client, calls
+`client.put(request_id, payload)` synchronously, and returns
+`{"durable": True, "id": request_id}` only after normal return. It ignores the
+return value of `put`. There is no wrapper-level journal, retry, exception handler,
+startup replay, or cancellation recovery. These observations do not establish
+missing end-to-end behavior: the driver or caller may supply it.
+
+Consider an honest remote service, process termination at each call boundary,
+restart, and a caller retrying with the same request ID and payload. Caller retry
+policy, remote persistence, acknowledgment semantics, and duplicate suppression
+are unknown. No permanent-wedge claim or security claim is made. Any eventual
+completion argument would additionally require service recovery and scheduled
+retries; neither is established here.
+
+## Coverage
+
+| Selected case | Code evidence and assessment | Limit |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Crash before `put` | Wrapper has not reported success; no local acceptance record exists. | No evidence of a promise to recover an unacknowledged call or caller replay policy. |
+| Crash during `put`, including after remote commit before acknowledgment | Outcome may be ambiguous to the caller. | Duplicate-free retry depends on unavailable driver/service semantics. No counterexample demonstrated. |
+| Normal `put` return followed by reported `durable=true`, then restart | Wrapper unconditionally asserts durability after normal return. | Cannot determine whether return means durable remote commit, volatile enqueue, or a returned error/status. |
+| Crash after `put` returns but before caller receives success | A retry can reach `put` again with the same ID. | Request-ID deduplication scope, atomicity, retention, and restart persistence are undocumented. |
+| `put` or client construction raises | Exception propagates; wrapper does not return success. | Remote effects on ambiguous errors and external retry/recovery remain unknown. |
+
+Conditional concerns: a non-durable acknowledgment could produce false success
+and lost exports; absence of durable deduplication could duplicate an export after
+an ambiguous result. These are dependency questions, **not proven defects**.
+Passing a request ID alone proves neither concern nor protection. Ignoring a
+return value is only a defect if the driver's contract requires inspecting it.
+
+## Checks actually performed
+
+- Read the deployment contract, submission code, related application modules,
+  root architecture, payload specification, local-only gate, and import
+  requirement to establish scope and applicable constraints.
+- Searched application code, records, README, and instructions for `submit`,
+  `remote_export`, driver, durability, request-ID, and record references. No
+  in-checkout submission callers or direct recovery paths were found.
+- Searched tracked-looking project files for driver, export, test, dependency
+  manifest, lockfile, and nested instruction paths; no production driver or
+  runnable deployment test was found.
+- Ran `python3 -c 'import importlib.util; print("export_transport_driver:", importlib.util.find_spec("export_transport_driver"))'`:
+  output was `export_transport_driver: None`. An earlier `python` invocation
+  failed because that executable is absent; the Python 3 check succeeded.
+
+No production submission, crash injection, retry test, generated search, model,
+or mutation check was run. Existing single-writer claim evidence was not used or
+reverified; this path does not touch the local store in the available code.
+
+## Remaining limits and stopping condition
+
+Further assessment requires the deployed driver's pinned implementation or
+authoritative contract (including acknowledgment and error semantics), remote
+request-ID guarantees, caller retry/restart behavior, and a representative
+executable deployment endpoint or fixture. Overlapping retries, payload changes
+under a reused ID, cancellation, service crashes, and deduplication expiry remain
+untested. Obtain that evidence before attributing a failure to this deployment
+or choosing a repair. The available code inspection is complete for this
+submission boundary; dependency gaps remain explicit and are not acceptance of
+the risk or a guarantee of correctness.
